      "id": "D01-05",
      "doc": "m0-d01-program-targets.md",
      "rule": "A system boundary's Trust Services categories form a set of 1 to 5 distinct values drawn from security, availability, confidentiality, processing_integrity, and privacy, and the set must always contain security; boundary draft creation, revision, and successor proposal reject any other shape."
    },
    {
      "id": "D01-06",
      "doc": "m0-d01-program-targets.md",
      "rule": "Approving a system boundary draft re-validates it, so a draft recorded before the security rule existed cannot be approved without security, while earlier approved boundary versions remain readable history."
    },
    {
      "id": "D01-07",
      "doc": "m0-d01-program-targets.md",
      "rule": "Optional Trust Services categories are never inferred from a program name or target path; they must be recorded explicitly on the boundary, and if a selected category proves not applicable management revises the boundary through an attributed successor version."
    },
    {
      "id": "D01-09",
      "doc": "m0-d01-program-targets.md",
      "rule": "A system boundary may carry a typed, owned, unresolved service reference before the client service register exists, but it cannot claim a governed service identity from an arbitrary UUID; the governed service and its exact boundary relationship must be validated later."
    },
    {
      "id": "D22-09",
      "doc": "m0-d22-canonical-ownership.md",
      "rule": "Cross-context references use tenant ID, stable record ID, and an exact version when a decision or snapshot relies on the record; before an owning inventory exists a boundary may keep a typed unresolved reference with owner and rationale."
    }
  ],
  "decides": "Trust Services categories are a set of 1 to 5 distinct values from security, availability, confidentiality, processing_integrity and privacy that must contain security, enforced on draft creation, revision, successor proposal and approval (a stale draft without security cannot be approved, earlier approved versions stay readable). Optional categories are recorded explicitly and never inferred from a program name or target path; dropping one is a successor version. A typed owned unresolved service reference may be kept but cannot claim governed identity from an arbitrary UUID, and cross-context references carry tenant ID, record ID and exact version.",
  "decidesCovers": [
    "D01-05",
    "D01-06",
    "D01-07",
    "D01-09",
    "D22-09"
  ],
